<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dharmawangsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1016">1016</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Dharmawangsa Teguh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dharmawangsa Teguh was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medang-kingdom">Medang</a> kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a> from about 990 to 1016. Around 990 he attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> without lasting success. In 1016 his palace was destroyed by Haji Wurawari, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> probably backed by Srivijaya, and the king was killed; his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a> escaped and later founded the kingdom of Kahuripan.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Anjukladang inscription of 937 rewards a village for repelling invaders from Malayu, that is Srivijaya, and in 990 <b>Dharmawangsa</b> launched a naval invasion to capture Palembang: Java's envoys of 992 told the Chinese court of continuous war, and Srivijaya had recovered by 1003.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Javanese kingdom of Medang under <b>Dharmawangsa</b> attacked Sumatra around 990 CE, Srivijaya withstood the assault, and in 1016 the Medang court was destroyed by a rebellion of the vassal ruler of Wurawari, an event many historians attribute to Srivijayan backing or regard as a Srivijayan invasion…</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Haji Wurawari destroyed the court of <b>Dharmawangsa Teguh</b> of the Medang Kingdom in 1016, his young son-in-law Airlangga, a prince from Bali, fled to the forest with a few followers.</blockquote><a href="/state/kahuripan">Kahuripan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-7.31,109.42&amp;year=1006" aria-label="Java on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7578 2437 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dharmawangsa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8028 2707h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8028 2707h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-7.31,109.42&amp;year=1006">What was here in 1006? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
